WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:01.260
Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:03.880
My name is Sai Vennam, and I'm with the IBM Cloud team.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.160
Today let's talk about multicloud.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.100
Multicloud is a cloud computing approach made
up of 2 or more cloud environments.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.440
Let's get started.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:20.840
So, we've got a public environment here as
well as a private cloud.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.220
There's an important distinction to be made here:

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.140
multicloud is not exactly the same thing as hybrid cloud.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.260
Hybrid cloud implies that your workloads are
working together across multiple clouds

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.500
- so interoperability and portability of your workloads.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.760
Multicloud doesn't have that same requirement,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.120
but we are seeing that a lot of enterprise users and customers

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.460
are using multi and hybrid cloud strategy together.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.220
I'd say that the growth of containers and
Kubernetes technology

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:50.060
has really enabled the growth of multicloud.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:54.380
So, we're seeing almost every big cloud provider
out there providing managed Kubernetes,

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.660
as well as it being used on-premises in the private sector

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.360
to help modernize legacy applications.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.480
So, why would you want to use multicloud and
the strategy for your cloud computing?

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.500
Well, there's a number of reasons.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.900
For one, let's say that you want to hit that
big

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.500
three-nines number as far as availability goes.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.780
Multicloud strategy can help you hit that
by ensuring that, you know, it's one thing

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:23.720
when the clouds of one of your application
workloads stops to work you still have another

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.240
cloud that's supporting your applications.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.210
In addition, you can enable better user experience.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:33.250
So, let's say you have users on different
sides of the globe.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:38.759
By routing them to the cloud that's nearest
to them, you can ensure lower latency and

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:40.180
better user experiences.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:46.240
Finally, what about specific integrations
that only work on a particular clouds?

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.659
Say you have some sensitive firewall data
that you don't want to put in the public cloud,

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:54.159
but you need to build integrations for.

00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:59.130
By taking advantage of a multicloud approach,
you can build workloads on the private side

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.420
that can take advantage of that private, sensitive,
customer data.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:09.629
I'd say there's 3 major ways and things that
you want to tackle when taking on a multicloud

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.910
strategies, and I'm gonna introduce these
as three pillars.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:16.640
The first one is gonna be automation.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:25.670
So, to do this I, let me introduce kind two
different types of users: so, we've got Ops,

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.390
as well as Devs.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:34.840
So, operations engineers are going to want
to be able to spin up new clusters as well

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:38.770
as manage and, kind of, see the different
clusters that have been created.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:42.819
So, they're gonna want to take advantage of
a unified, kind of, dashboard to do something

00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:43.819
like that.

00:02:43.819 --> 00:02:49.379
So, we'll have the control plane here.

00:02:49.379 --> 00:02:54.510
Let's say the ops engineers want to spin up
two Kubernetes clusters.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:03:01.980
So, they'll essentially figure out that configuration,
and then go to the control panel, and tell

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.099
it to spin up those clusters.

00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:07.540
At that point you know they can specify where
they want those clusters to be created.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:11.310
Let's say they spin up two.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:16.610
Kubernetes clusters, one in each of the clouds.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:22.330
On, the other side with devs, let's say that
they've built out a new application, a Docker

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:25.160
container, and they're ready to push it.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:32.580
So, they've got a container built, and they
wanted to deploy it the same way to both of

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:37.349
the clouds but you know it can be quite difficult
if they didn't have a control plane to figure

00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:42.120
out the different authentications and configurations
of the unique different clouds and kind of

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.570
deploy that both times.

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:48.670
In addition, this really gets in the way of
traditional kind of CICD and DevOps workflows.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:53.470
So, by taking advantage of that control plane,
they can spin up that container in both of

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:58.049
the clouds with a single command.

00:03:58.049 --> 00:04:00.720
Say that it's application 'A'.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.450
So, I think that really hits this requirement
for automation with multicloud.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:16.420
The next kind of requirement that I want to
hit on here is visibility.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:20.440
Visibility is very important for working with
multiple clouds because, you know, they each

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:25.800
have their own unique dashboards but to have
a single unified way of managing those things

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.110
is very important.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:33.470
So, for operations engineers, by taking advantage
of a single control dashboard, they can have

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:39.530
access to a unified kind of approach and see
all the different, you know, clusters that

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:44.140
they've spun up and even drill down deeper
and see things like pods and deployments in

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:48.980
applications across multiple clouds.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:57.140
The last thing I want to touch on, the last
pillar, is governance.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:01.980
These days regulatory and compliance policies
are becoming increasingly strict and they

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:04.670
differ from geography to geography.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:09.010
Let's say the operations team has a compliance
policy that they want to push out across all

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:10.690
of these clouds.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:17.420
So, first they'll have to, you know, write
out that compliance policy, and then, you

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:21.800
know, if they didn't have access to a control
plane, this can be a very kind of painstaking,

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:23.360
or rather time-consuming, approach.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:29.580
So, by taking advantage of the control plane
they can push that compliance policy across

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:35.390
multiple clusters with a single command and
ensure those security policies for compliance

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:36.390
are being met.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:42.460
I'd say that these are the 3 major things
that you want to tackle when taking on a multicloud

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:43.480
strategy.

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:45.830
Multicloud has been a growing focus for us
here at IBM.

